.TH ASTYLES 4
.SH NAME
astyles \- layers of color in acme's windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B astyles
[
.B -Di
] [
.B -a
.I acme
] [
.B -m
.I mtpt
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Astyles
lets more than one program color the text of one
.IR acme (1)
window at once.
Each program writes its own layer, and
.I astyles
stacks the layers and writes the result to the window's
.B style
file (see
.IR acme (4)).
It posts itself as
.BI /srv/ srvname
.RB ( /srv/astyles
by default) and mounts itself on
.I mtpt
.RB ( /mnt/astyles ),
then waits for
.BR $acmesrv ,
the
.B /srv
file of an acme
(which
.B acmestart
sets),
and follows that acme's windows, mounted on
.I acme
.RB ( /mnt/acme ).
It exits when acme does.
Started just before acme, as
.B acmestart
starts it, it shares the namespace acme starts in,
so every command acme runs finds it at
.BR /mnt/astyles .
The
.B -D
option prints the 9P messages;
.B -i
serves 9P on standard input and output, and posts and mounts nothing.
.PP
The top directory has a directory for each of acme's windows, named by the window's ID.
Each holds:
.TF layers/new
.TP
.B style
The window's layers stacked, in the format of acme's
.B style
file: what
.I astyles
last composed.
.TP
.B layers/new
Opening it makes a new layer and reading it gives the layer's number.
.TP
.B layers/index
A line for each layer: its number and its name.
.TP
.BI layers/ n
The directory of layer
.IR n .
.PD
.PP
A layer's directory holds:
.TF style
.TP
.B name
The layer's name, which places it in the stack; a new layer's is its number.
Write a word to rename it.
.TP
.B style
The layer's text, in the format of acme's
.B style
file and
.IR theme (6):
lines naming colors, then runs, each a start and length in runes and a name.
A write takes effect when its program closes the file,
and replaces the layer's colors and runs;
but after a write to
.BR addr ,
it replaces only the runs at the address, its runs' starts are relative to it,
and it adds its colors to the layer's.
Reading gives the layer's colors and runs.
.TP
.B addr
Write two numbers, the start and end in runes of the text the next write of
.B style
replaces.
As acme's
.B addr
file does, an open with no other open resets it.
.TP
.B seq
Reading gives the number of edits made to the window's body.
Writing a number guards the next open of
.B style
for writing: if anyone has edited the body since, the open fails with
.LR "seq mismatch" ;
and
.I astyles
drops a write closed after an edit.
The number is the one acme's own
.B seq
file for the window gives, so a program that read the body with a guard can write its runs with the same number.
.TP
.B ctl
.L clear
empties the layer;
.L delete
removes it.
.PD
.PP
A layer lasts until a program deletes it or its window closes.
A program looks its layer up by name in
.B index
and makes one with
.B new
if it has none.
.SS Stacking
Where layers' runs overlap, the higher layer's run shows, color by color:
the text takes the highest run's text color, and the background the highest run's background,
so a run that gives only a background over a keyword keeps the keyword's text color.
A run takes its colors from its layer's lines, or else from the theme.
The first of
.B $home/lib/acme/layers
and
.B /lib/acme/layers
lists the layers' names, top first, one to a line;
a
.L *
places every layer the file does not name, and a
.L #
starts a comment.
Of two layers in the same place, the newer is higher.
.I Astyles
reads the file when it starts.
.SS Colors
.I Astyles
looks a run's name up in its own layer's colors, then in the theme
.RI ( theme (6)).
It leaves out a run whose name is in neither, so the layers under it show through.
Where two layers give one name different colors,
.I astyles
writes one under the name with its layer's number added
.RB ( keyword.3 ),
so each layer's runs keep their colors;
a color made from two layers', as under
.BR Stacking ,
has
.B .m
and a number added, when its name has another color in the window.
.PP
A color in a layer's lines may have two more hexadecimal digits,
.IR rrggbbaa ,
its opacity:
.I astyles
blends it over the color beneath, the next layer's, or else the theme's
.B body
or acme's own, and writes acme the opaque result;
reading the layer gives the color as written.
The theme's colors are six digits, as
.IR theme (6)
has them.
.SS Edits
.I Astyles
follows each window's edits in its
.B rdevent
file.
A layer's runs move with text inserted or deleted before them,
and a run grows to cover text typed inside it, until its program writes the layer again.
.I Get
reloads the body without logging its edits, so it empties every layer of the window.
.I Astyles
writes to acme's
.B style
file a moment after a change, only the text that changed,
under acme's
.B seq
guard.
.SH EXAMPLES
Color the first word of window 3 in a layer named
.BR mark :
.IP
.EX
l=`{cat /mnt/astyles/3/layers/new}
echo mark >/mnt/astyles/3/layers/$l/name
echo '0 5 highlight' >/mnt/astyles/3/layers/$l/style
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF $home/lib/acme/layers
.TP
.B /lib/acme/layers
the order of the layers
.TP
.B $home/lib/acme/layers
the user's order, read in place of
.B /lib/acme/layers
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/astyles.c
.br
.B rc/bin/acmestart
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR theme (6)
.SH BUGS
A partial write of a window's styles sets acme's
.B addr
for the window, which a program editing the window by
.B addr
and
.B data
may have just set; see
.IR acme (4).
